{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"doubly_linked","title":"Doubly Linked List","group":"Linked Lists","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A doubly linked list gives every node a <strong><code>prev</code></strong> pointer in addition to <code>next</code>, so the list can be walked in both directions and any node can be removed in O(1) given only a reference to it. It costs one extra pointer per node and a little more bookkeeping.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Doubly linked list</strong> — a linked list whose nodes hold <code>data</code>, <code>next</code> (the following node) and <code>prev</code> (the preceding node). The head's <code>prev</code> and the tail's <code>next</code> are <code>None</code>. Every link is mirrored: <code>a.next == b</code> always implies <code>b.prev == a</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A doubly linked list: every node knows both its neighbours</figcaption><pre><code>         head                                                   tail\n          ↓                                                       ↓\n     ┌────┬──────┬────┐     ┌────┬──────┬────┐     ┌────┬──────┬────┐\n∅ ◀──┼ ●  │  10  │ ●──┼────▶│ ●  │  20  │ ●──┼────▶│ ●  │  30  │ ●──┼──▶ ∅\n     └────┴──────┴────┘◀────┼─●  │      │    │◀────┼─●  │      │    │\n      prev  data  next      └────┴──────┴────┘     └────┴──────┴────┘\n\nNone &lt;-&gt; 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30 &lt;-&gt; None</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Structure of a node and traversal in both directions</h3>\n<p>Linking two nodes now means setting <strong>two</strong> pointers: <code>a.next = b</code> <em>and</em> <code>b.prev = a</code>. Forgetting one of them is the classic doubly-linked bug — the list looks fine going forward and breaks going backward. Traversal backwards starts from the last node and follows <code>prev</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts, curr, last = [], head, None\n    while curr is not None:\n        parts.append(str(curr.data))\n        last = curr                          # remember the tail for the way back\n        curr = curr.next\n    print(\"forward:\", \" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts))\n    parts, curr = [], last\n    while curr is not None:                  # walk back along prev\n        parts.append(str(curr.data))\n        curr = curr.prev\n    print(\"backward:\", \" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = Node(10); temp1 = Node(20); temp2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = temp1;  temp1.prev = head        # every link is set in both directions\ntemp1.next = temp2; temp2.prev = temp1\nshow(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    Node* last = nullptr;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"forward:\";\n    for (Node* curr = head; curr; curr = curr-&gt;next) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; (curr == head ? \" \" : \" &lt;-&gt; \") &lt;&lt; curr-&gt;data;\n        last = curr;                             // remember the tail for the way back\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\nbackward:\";\n    for (Node* curr = last; curr; curr = curr-&gt;prev)   // walk back along prev\n        cout &lt;&lt; (curr == last ? \" \" : \" &lt;-&gt; \") &lt;&lt; curr-&gt;data;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* temp1 = new Node(20); Node* temp2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = temp1;  temp1-&gt;prev = head;     // every link is set in both directions\n    temp1-&gt;next = temp2; temp2-&gt;prev = temp1;\n    show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"forward:\");\n        Node last = null;\n        for (Node curr = head; curr != null; curr = curr.next) {\n            sb.append(curr == head ? \" \" : \" &lt;-&gt; \").append(curr.data);\n            last = curr;                             // remember the tail for the way back\n        }\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        sb = new StringBuilder(\"backward:\");\n        for (Node curr = last; curr != null; curr = curr.prev)   // walk back along prev\n            sb.append(curr == last ? \" \" : \" &lt;-&gt; \").append(curr.data);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), temp1 = new Node(20), temp2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = temp1;  temp1.prev = head;       // every link is set in both directions\n        temp1.next = temp2; temp2.prev = temp1;\n        show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    let last = null;\n    for (let curr = head; curr !== null; curr = curr.next) {\n        parts.push(curr.data);\n        last = curr;                                 // remember the tail for the way back\n    }\n    console.log(\"forward:\", parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \"));\n    const back = [];\n    for (let curr = last; curr !== null; curr = curr.prev)   // walk back along prev\n        back.push(curr.data);\n    console.log(\"backward:\", back.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \"));\n}\n\nconst head = new Node(10), temp1 = new Node(20), temp2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = temp1;  temp1.prev = head;               // every link is set in both directions\ntemp1.next = temp2; temp2.prev = temp1;\nshow(head);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>forward: 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\nbackward: 30 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) traversal</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for n nodes</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>Advantages and disadvantages</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>Traversal in both directions</h3><p>Walk forward with <code>next</code>, backward with <code>prev</code> — browsers' back/forward history, text editor cursors, undo/redo.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Delete a node in O(1) given its reference</h3><p><code>node.prev.next = node.next; node.next.prev = node.prev</code>. A singly linked list would first need an O(n) walk to find the predecessor.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-left\"></i>Insert / delete before a given node</h3><p>The predecessor is one pointer away, so inserting <em>before</em> is as cheap as inserting after.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i>O(1) at both ends with a tail</h3><p>Keeping <code>tail</code> makes insert and delete at <strong>both</strong> ends O(1) — the basis of <code>deque</code> and the LRU cache.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Extra space</h3><p>A second pointer per node: 16 bytes of links per element on a 64-bit machine.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>More complex code</h3><p>Every operation updates up to four pointers and must keep <code>prev</code>/<code>next</code> consistent; more special cases for the head and tail.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Singly linked</th><th>Doubly linked</th><th>Why the difference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Insert at beginning</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Both just re-point the head; doubly also sets the old head's <code>prev</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Insert at end</td><td>O(n) / O(1) with tail</td><td>O(n) / O(1) with tail</td><td>Same walk unless a tail is kept.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete head</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Doubly also clears the new head's <code>prev</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete last</td><td>O(n) even with tail</td><td><strong>O(1) with tail</strong></td><td>The tail's <code>prev</code> gives the new last node for free.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete a given node</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>The predecessor is <code>node.prev</code>; singly must search for it.</td></tr><tr><td>Insert before a given node</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>Same reason.</td></tr><tr><td>Reverse</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Doubly: swap <code>prev</code> and <code>next</code> in every node.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-left\"></i>Insert at the beginning</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30, insert 5 at the beginning</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>5 &lt;-&gt; 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Point the new node's <code>next</code> at the old head, point the old head's <code>prev</code> back at the new node, and return the new node as head. Two pointer writes — O(1). The empty list needs a guard: there is no old head whose <code>prev</code> could be set (the original notes crashed on this case).</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef insert_at_beginning(head, data):\n    temp = Node(data)\n    temp.next = head                 # new → old head\n    if head is not None:\n        head.prev = temp             # old head → new (guard the empty list)\n    return temp\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(\" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nshow(head)\nhead = insert_at_beginning(head, 5); show(head)\nhead = insert_at_beginning(head, 1); show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* insertAtBeginning(Node* head, int data) {\n    Node* temp = new Node(data);\n    temp-&gt;next = head;               // new → old head\n    if (head) head-&gt;prev = temp;     // old head → new (guard the empty list)\n    return temp;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    show(head);\n    head = insertAtBeginning(head, 5); show(head);\n    head = insertAtBeginning(head, 1); show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node insertAtBeginning(Node head, int data) {\n        Node temp = new Node(data);\n        temp.next = head;                    // new → old head\n        if (head != null) head.prev = temp;  // old head → new (guard the empty list)\n        return temp;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        show(head);\n        head = insertAtBeginning(head, 5); show(head);\n        head = insertAtBeginning(head, 1); show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction insertAtBeginning(head, data) {\n    const temp = new Node(data);\n    temp.next = head;                        // new → old head\n    if (head !== null) head.prev = temp;     // old head → new (guard the empty list)\n    return temp;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \"));\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nshow(head);\nhead = insertAtBeginning(head, 5); show(head);\nhead = insertAtBeginning(head, 1); show(head);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\n5 &lt;-&gt; 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\n1 &lt;-&gt; 5 &lt;-&gt; 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-right\"></i>Insert at the end</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30, insert 40 at the end</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30 &lt;-&gt; 40</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = empty, insert 40</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>40</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk to the last node, link it forward to the new node and link the new node back with <code>prev</code>. O(n) because of the walk; a class that stores <code>tail</code> does the same two writes in O(1).</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef insert_end(head, x):\n    temp = Node(x)\n    if head is None:\n        return temp                      # empty list: new node is the head\n    curr = head\n    while curr.next is not None:         # O(n) walk to the last node\n        curr = curr.next\n    curr.next = temp                     # last → new\n    temp.prev = curr                     # new → last\n    return head\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(\" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nhead = insert_end(head, 40); show(head)\nempty = insert_end(None, 40); show(empty)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* insertEnd(Node* head, int x) {\n    Node* temp = new Node(x);\n    if (!head) return temp;                  // empty list: new node is the head\n    Node* curr = head;\n    while (curr-&gt;next) curr = curr-&gt;next;    // O(n) walk to the last node\n    curr-&gt;next = temp;                       // last → new\n    temp-&gt;prev = curr;                       // new → last\n    return head;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    head = insertEnd(head, 40); show(head);\n    Node* empty = insertEnd(nullptr, 40); show(empty);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node insertEnd(Node head, int x) {\n        Node temp = new Node(x);\n        if (head == null) return temp;               // empty list: new node is the head\n        Node curr = head;\n        while (curr.next != null) curr = curr.next;  // O(n) walk to the last node\n        curr.next = temp;                            // last → new\n        temp.prev = curr;                            // new → last\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        head = insertEnd(head, 40); show(head);\n        Node empty = insertEnd(null, 40); show(empty);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction insertEnd(head, x) {\n    const temp = new Node(x);\n    if (head === null) return temp;                  // empty list: new node is the head\n    let curr = head;\n    while (curr.next !== null) curr = curr.next;     // O(n) walk to the last node\n    curr.next = temp;                                // last → new\n    temp.prev = curr;                                // new → last\n    return head;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \"));\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nhead = insertEnd(head, 40); show(head);\nconst empty = insertEnd(null, 40); show(empty);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30 &lt;-&gt; 40\n40</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) — O(1) with a tail pointer</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-left\"></i>Delete the head</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>empty</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Move the head to the second node and clear its <code>prev</code> — without that step the new head would still point back at a node that is no longer in the list. A one-node list becomes empty.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef delete_head(head):\n    if head is None or head.next is None:\n        return None                      # 0 or 1 node → empty\n    head = head.next\n    head.prev = None                     # the new head has no predecessor\n    return head\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(\" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts) if parts else \"empty\")\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nfor _ in range(3):\n    head = delete_head(head)\n    print(\"after delete head: \", end=\"\"); show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* deleteHead(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) return nullptr;\n    if (!head-&gt;next) { delete head; return nullptr; }   // 1 node → empty\n    Node* newHead = head-&gt;next;\n    newHead-&gt;prev = nullptr;                 // the new head has no predecessor\n    delete head;\n    return newHead;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) { cout &lt;&lt; \"empty\\n\"; return; }\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n        head = deleteHead(head);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete head: \"; show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node deleteHead(Node head) {\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) return null;   // 0 or 1 node → empty\n        head = head.next;\n        head.prev = null;                        // the new head has no predecessor\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) { System.out.println(\"empty\"); return; }\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n            head = deleteHead(head);\n            System.out.print(\"after delete head: \"); show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction deleteHead(head) {\n    if (head === null || head.next === null) return null;    // 0 or 1 node → empty\n    head = head.next;\n    head.prev = null;                            // the new head has no predecessor\n    return head;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.length ? parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \") : \"empty\");\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n    head = deleteHead(head);\n    process.stdout.write(\"after delete head: \"); show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>after delete head: 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\nafter delete head: 30\nafter delete head: empty</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-right\"></i>Delete the last node</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 &lt;-&gt; 20</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>empty</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk to the last node and cut it off via <code>last.prev.next = None</code>. Thanks to <code>prev</code> the predecessor is one hop away; the only O(n) part is reaching the last node, which disappears when a <code>tail</code> pointer is kept — the operation a singly linked list cannot do in O(1) even with a tail.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef delete_last(head):\n    if head is None or head.next is None:\n        return None                      # 0 or 1 node → empty\n    curr = head\n    while curr.next is not None:         # walk to the last node (O(1) with a tail pointer)\n        curr = curr.next\n    curr.prev.next = None                # predecessor is one hop away via prev\n    return head\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(\" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts) if parts else \"empty\")\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nfor _ in range(3):\n    head = delete_last(head)\n    print(\"after delete last: \", end=\"\"); show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* deleteLast(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) return nullptr;\n    if (!head-&gt;next) { delete head; return nullptr; }   // 1 node → empty\n    Node* curr = head;\n    while (curr-&gt;next) curr = curr-&gt;next;    // walk to the last node (O(1) with a tail pointer)\n    curr-&gt;prev-&gt;next = nullptr;              // predecessor is one hop away via prev\n    delete curr;\n    return head;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) { cout &lt;&lt; \"empty\\n\"; return; }\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n        head = deleteLast(head);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete last: \"; show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node deleteLast(Node head) {\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) return null;   // 0 or 1 node → empty\n        Node curr = head;\n        while (curr.next != null) curr = curr.next;   // walk to the last node (O(1) with a tail pointer)\n        curr.prev.next = null;                        // predecessor is one hop away via prev\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) { System.out.println(\"empty\"); return; }\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n            head = deleteLast(head);\n            System.out.print(\"after delete last: \"); show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction deleteLast(head) {\n    if (head === null || head.next === null) return null;    // 0 or 1 node → empty\n    let curr = head;\n    while (curr.next !== null) curr = curr.next;     // walk to the last node (O(1) with a tail pointer)\n    curr.prev.next = null;                           // predecessor is one hop away via prev\n    return head;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.length ? parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \") : \"empty\");\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n    head = deleteLast(head);\n    process.stdout.write(\"after delete last: \"); show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>after delete last: 10 &lt;-&gt; 20\nafter delete last: 10\nafter delete last: empty</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) — O(1) with a tail pointer</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Delete a node given its reference</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30, node = the node holding 20</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 &lt;-&gt; 30</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>This is the operation that justifies the extra pointer. With <code>node.prev</code> and <code>node.next</code> both known, unlink in O(1): the predecessor's <code>next</code> skips to the successor and the successor's <code>prev</code> skips back to the predecessor. Guard the ends: if <code>node</code> is the head there is no predecessor (the head moves), if it is the tail there is no successor.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Deleting a node in O(1): two pointer writes on its neighbours</figcaption><pre><code>before:   [10] ◀──▶ [20] ◀──▶ [30]\n                    node\n\nnode.prev.next = node.next     [10] ───────▶ [30]       (10's next skips 20)\nnode.next.prev = node.prev     [10] ◀─────── [30]       (30's prev skips 20)\n\nafter:    [10] ◀──▶ [30]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef delete_node(head, node):\n    if node.prev is not None:\n        node.prev.next = node.next       # predecessor skips over node\n    else:\n        head = node.next                 # node was the head\n    if node.next is not None:\n        node.next.prev = node.prev       # successor skips back over node\n    return head\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(\" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts) if parts else \"empty\")\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nshow(head)\nhead = delete_node(head, t1); print(\"delete node 20: \", end=\"\"); show(head)\nhead = delete_node(head, head); print(\"delete node 10 (head): \", end=\"\"); show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* deleteNode(Node* head, Node* node) {\n    if (node-&gt;prev) node-&gt;prev-&gt;next = node-&gt;next;   // predecessor skips over node\n    else head = node-&gt;next;                          // node was the head\n    if (node-&gt;next) node-&gt;next-&gt;prev = node-&gt;prev;   // successor skips back over node\n    delete node;\n    return head;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) { cout &lt;&lt; \"empty\\n\"; return; }\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    show(head);\n    head = deleteNode(head, t1); cout &lt;&lt; \"delete node 20: \"; show(head);\n    head = deleteNode(head, head); cout &lt;&lt; \"delete node 10 (head): \"; show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node deleteNode(Node head, Node node) {\n        if (node.prev != null) node.prev.next = node.next;   // predecessor skips over node\n        else head = node.next;                               // node was the head\n        if (node.next != null) node.next.prev = node.prev;   // successor skips back over node\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) { System.out.println(\"empty\"); return; }\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        show(head);\n        head = deleteNode(head, t1); System.out.print(\"delete node 20: \"); show(head);\n        head = deleteNode(head, head); System.out.print(\"delete node 10 (head): \"); show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction deleteNode(head, node) {\n    if (node.prev !== null) node.prev.next = node.next;   // predecessor skips over node\n    else head = node.next;                                // node was the head\n    if (node.next !== null) node.next.prev = node.prev;   // successor skips back over node\n    return head;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.length ? parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \") : \"empty\");\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nshow(head);\nhead = deleteNode(head, t1); process.stdout.write(\"delete node 20: \"); show(head);\nhead = deleteNode(head, head); process.stdout.write(\"delete node 10 (head): \"); show(head);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\ndelete node 20: 10 &lt;-&gt; 30\ndelete node 10 (head): 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>This is exactly how an <strong>LRU cache</strong> evicts and promotes entries: a hash map gives the node for a key in O(1), and the doubly linked list moves that node to the front in O(1). Neither structure alone can do both.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Reverse a doubly linked list</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>30 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 10</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Reversing means every node's <code>next</code> becomes its <code>prev</code> and vice versa — so <strong>swap the two pointers in each node</strong>. After the swap, the <em>old</em> <code>next</code> is reachable as <code>curr.prev</code>, which is how the loop advances. The last node visited is the new head.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Walk the list</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>curr = head</code>, remembering the node just processed in <code>prev</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Swap in place</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>curr.next, curr.prev = curr.prev, curr.next</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Advance</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>curr = curr.prev</code> — that field now holds the old <code>next</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Finish</div><div class=\"step-desc\">When <code>curr</code> is <code>None</code>, <code>prev</code> is the last node processed: the new head.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\ndef reverse_dll(head):\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr is not None:\n        prev = curr\n        curr.next, curr.prev = curr.prev, curr.next   # swap the two links\n        curr = curr.prev                              # old next now lives in prev\n    return prev                                       # last node processed = new head\n\ndef show(label, head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data)); head = head.next\n    print(label + \":\", \" &lt;-&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = Node(10); t1 = Node(20); t2 = Node(30)\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1\nshow(\"forward\", head)\nhead = reverse_dll(head); show(\"reversed\", head)\nhead = reverse_dll(head); show(\"back again\", head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* prev; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* reverseDLL(Node* head) {\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    for (Node* curr = head; curr; curr = curr-&gt;prev) {   // after the swap, prev holds the old next\n        prev = curr;\n        swap(curr-&gt;next, curr-&gt;prev);                      // swap the two links\n    }\n    return prev;                                           // last node processed = new head\n}\n\nvoid show(const char* label, Node* head) {\n    cout &lt;&lt; label &lt;&lt; \":\";\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" &lt;-&gt;\" : \"\");\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); Node* t1 = new Node(20); Node* t2 = new Node(30);\n    head-&gt;next = t1; t1-&gt;prev = head; t1-&gt;next = t2; t2-&gt;prev = t1;\n    show(\"forward\", head);\n    head = reverseDLL(head); show(\"reversed\", head);\n    head = reverseDLL(head); show(\"back again\", head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node prev, next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node reverseDLL(Node head) {\n        Node prev = null;\n        for (Node curr = head; curr != null; curr = curr.prev) {   // after the swap, prev holds the old next\n            prev = curr;\n            Node t = curr.next; curr.next = curr.prev; curr.prev = t;   // swap the two links\n        }\n        return prev;                                               // last node processed = new head\n    }\n\n    static void show(String label, Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(label + \":\");\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(\" \").append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" &lt;-&gt;\" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\n        head.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\n        show(\"forward\", head);\n        head = reverseDLL(head); show(\"reversed\", head);\n        head = reverseDLL(head); show(\"back again\", head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.prev = null; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction reverseDLL(head) {\n    let prev = null;\n    for (let curr = head; curr !== null; curr = curr.prev) {   // after the swap, prev holds the old next\n        prev = curr;\n        [curr.next, curr.prev] = [curr.prev, curr.next];        // swap the two links\n    }\n    return prev;                                                // last node processed = new head\n}\n\nfunction show(label, head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(label + \":\", parts.join(\" &lt;-&gt; \"));\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10), t1 = new Node(20), t2 = new Node(30);\nhead.next = t1; t1.prev = head; t1.next = t2; t2.prev = t1;\nshow(\"forward\", head);\nhead = reverseDLL(head); show(\"reversed\", head);\nhead = reverseDLL(head); show(\"back again\", head);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>forward: 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30\nreversed: 30 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 10\nback again: 10 &lt;-&gt; 20 &lt;-&gt; 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Advancing with <code>curr = curr.next</code> <em>after</em> the swap walks backwards into the part already reversed and loops forever (or stops after one node). The old <code>next</code> is in <code>curr.prev</code> once the swap has happened.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does the extra prev pointer buy you?</summary><div><p>O(1) deletion of a node you hold a reference to, O(1) insertion <em>before</em> a node, O(1) removal from the tail (with a tail pointer) and backward traversal. The price is one more pointer per node and twice as many links to keep consistent.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does an LRU cache use a doubly linked list?</summary><div><p>Entries are nodes ordered from most to least recently used; a hash map points from each key to its node. On access, the node is unlinked in O(1) (possible only because of <code>prev</code>) and re-inserted at the front. On eviction the tail node is removed in O(1). All operations are O(1).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Singly vs doubly linked list — how do you choose?</summary><div><p>Singly when you only move forward and operate at the head (stacks, hash chains, adjacency lists) — it is smaller and simpler. Doubly when you need deletions in the middle by reference, both-end operations (deques) or backward iteration. Java's <code>LinkedList</code> and C++ <code>std::list</code> are doubly linked; <code>std::forward_list</code> is singly linked.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you reverse a doubly linked list?</summary><div><p>Swap <code>next</code> and <code>prev</code> in every node and return the last node visited. One pass, no extra memory. Because the old <code>next</code> ends up in <code>prev</code>, the loop advances with <code>curr = curr.prev</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is an XOR linked list?</summary><div><p>A memory-saving trick: each node stores <code>prev XOR next</code> in a single field. Walking forward from a known predecessor, <code>next = stored XOR prev</code>. It halves the pointer overhead but defeats garbage collectors and makes the code fragile — an interview curiosity rather than a production structure.</p></div></details></section>"}